t22918@ursa.calvin.edu (Matt Ranney) writes:
>I'm tring to compile 3.0 on a Tektronix something or other running sorta BSD.
>.. but 3.0 is giving me some problems.  The first few modules go through
>ok, but when it comes to onames.h, (I think) the file thats like 300
>lines of #defines, I get this lovely error:
>ccom: onames.h: line 75 - too much defining.

Ooh. Ick.  I (and several others too) encounterd this one when 3.0 was first
released way back.  It happens on a lot of machines.

>Is there any way to get around all these defines?
>The compiler is far from robust,

Well it's not the compiler that's troubling you.  It's your C preprocessor
that is barfing on all of those defines.

Possible solutions are #define STUPID_CPP in whatever header file it's in
in the include directory (that should help some), or if your case is terminal
(ie still won't work after that) try sniffing around for a PD
C compiler, and see if you machine will compile that.  Beg to the ghods of
nethack-bugs@linc.cis.upenn.edu.  They might have a few ideas about
preprocessors too.

There's *got* to be a way.  Good luck.

